tly low but  estrogen is actually important for  muscle growth with research suggesting  somewhere in the world of 24 to 30 body  fat as the sweet spot for healthy levels  so i'm just gonna operate with the  assumption that her estrogen levels are  okay but considering her other life goal  is to get pregnant and that her  progesterone levels are already low i do  worry about the hormonal tax of a recomp  like this i spoke to my colleague sports  dietitian chelsea cross who suggested  that in situations like this she would  encourage starting with like a health  phase where the focus would be on  optimizing hormones before trying to  manipulate macros to build muscle if our  hormones are not at an optimal place our  body is going to resist growth and i'm  going to discuss this in a little bit  more detail shortly but let's start by  talking about cassie's proposed diet in  general so cassie's sample meal plan in  her kickoff blog post has just over 1500  calories with 41 protein 19 fat and 43  carbs that puts her at 1.25 grams of  protein per pound of body weight which  is within the range of what we discussed  as optimal in my video here now i'm also  seeing her protein spread throughout the  day in five smallish meals first of all  i am definitely glad to see that we're  not trying to squeeze meals into like a  strict fasting regimen and that we're  spacing meals out throughout the day i'm  gonna be discussing this in a lot more  detail in an upcoming video but in short  when we're talking about women who are  dieting or engaging in a lot of exercise  we worry about what we call a state of  low energy availability and that's the  energy left for optimizing health after  you account for calories consumed and  exercise what we know is that for women  more hours a day spent with an energy  availability below 300 calories is  associated with menstrual dysfunction  and hormonal irregularities which as  we've established is not ideal for  muscle growth either so this is 